1. Non-Standard Web Domains (.onion)
Instead of standard domain extensions (.com, .org) resolved by public DNS servers, dark web sites use custom cryptographic addresses (e.g., [http://expyuz4...onion](http://expyuz4...onion)). These addresses can only be routed through the Tor network.

2. Onion Routing (Multi-Hop Encryption)
When accessing a .onion site, your connection is wrapped in multiple layers of encryption and bounced through three randomized volunteer servers called nodes:

Guard/Entry Node: Sees your real IP address, but cannot see where you are going or what data you are sending.

Middle Node: Knows only the IP addresses of the node before it and after it; holds no identity or traffic details.

Exit/Rendezvous Node: Delivers traffic to the final destination without ever knowing your original IP address.

3. Cryptocurrency Transactions
Because traditional banking involves identity verification (KYC), transactions on dark web marketplaces rely heavily on privacy-focused cryptocurrencies (such as Monero or Bitcoin) to maintain transaction anonymity.
